# Security
- Store sensitive data securely using the flutter_secure_storage package.
- Avoid hardcoding API keys; use environment variables or secure storage.
- Validate all user inputs to prevent injection attacks.
- Use HTTPS for all network communications.
- Regularly update dependencies to patch known vulnerabilities.

# Performance
- Minimize widget rebuilds by using const constructors where possible.
- Use ListView.builder for large lists to improve performance.
- Avoid unnecessary computations in the build method.
- Use the const keyword to create compile-time constants.
- Profile your app regularly to identify and fix performance bottlenecks.

# Navigation
- Use the Navigator 2.0 API for declarative routing.
- Avoid deep linking directly to widget constructors; use named routes.
- Manage navigation state using a centralized navigation service.
- Ensure that navigation transitions are smooth and intuitive.
- Use the `pushReplacement` method to replace routes when necessary.

# UI Design
- Follow Material Design guidelines for consistent and visually appealing interfaces.
- Utilize Flutter's built-in widgets to maintain consistency across platforms.
- Ensure responsiveness by using MediaQuery and LayoutBuilder for adaptive layouts.
- Optimize images and assets to reduce app size and improve load times.
- Test your UI across different devices and screen sizes to ensure compatibility.

# Code Quality
- Follow the Dart style guide for consistent code formatting.
- Use the provider package for state management.
- Keep widget trees shallow to improve readability and performance.
- Document all public classes and methods with Dartdoc comments.

# Architecture
- Follow the Model-View-ViewModel (MVVM) design pattern.
- Use repositories to manage data sources.
- Keep business logic separate from UI code.
- Use dependency injection to manage dependencies.
- Structure your project into feature modules for scalability.

# Testing
- Write widget tests to ensure UI components function correctly.
- Use mock data for unit tests to isolate functionality.
- Test asynchronous code using the `test` package's async utilities.
- Ensure that tests are fast and reliable.
- Run tests on multiple devices and screen sizes to ensure compatibility.

# Accessibility
- Ensure all interactive elements are accessible.
- Use semantic widgets to improve screen reader support.
- Provide alternative text for images.
- Test your app with accessibility tools to identify issues.
- Follow the WCAG guidelines for mobile accessibility.

# Localization
- Use the intl package for internationalization.
- Provide translations for all user-facing strings.
- Test your app in different languages to ensure proper layout.
- Handle right-to-left languages appropriately.
- Ensure that date, time, and number formats are locale-aware.

# Error Handling
- Use try-catch blocks to handle exceptions gracefully.
- Log errors using a logging package for better debugging.
- Provide user-friendly error messages.
- Avoid exposing stack traces to end-users.
- Monitor app crashes using services like Firebase Crashlytics.

# Dependency Management
- Use the pubspec.yaml file to manage dependencies.
- Avoid using outdated or unmaintained packages.
- Regularly run `flutter pub outdated` to check for newer versions.
- Use dependency overrides cautiously.
- Keep your dependencies up to date to benefit from the latest features and security patches.
